          %h2#ModelCategoryTheory Abstract homotopy theory
          %p
            In the
            %a{:href => "#BackgroundOnTopologicalHomotopyTheoryFromAlgebraicTopology"} above
            we discussed three classes of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"}> weak homotopy equivalences
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+cell+complexes"}> relative cell complexes
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+fibrations"} Serre fibrations
          %p
            and we saw first aspects of their interplay via
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting+properties"} lifting properties
          %p
            A fundamental insight due to (
            %a{:href => "#Quillen67"}> Quillen 67
            ) is that in fact
            %em all
            constructions in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            are elegantly expressible via just the abstract interplay of these classes of morphisms. This was distilled in (
            %a{:href => "#Quillen67"}> Quillen 67
            ) into a small set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            called a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              structure
            (because it serves to make all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            be
            %em models
            for
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
          %p
            This
            %em abstract homotopy theory
            is the royal road for handling any flavor of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            that we are after in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+1"} Part 1
            %a{:href => "#TheClassicalModelStructureOfTopologicalSpaces"} below
            we conclude this
            %em Introduction to Homotopy Theory
            by showing that topological spaces equipped with the above system of classes continuous functions is indeed an example of
            %em abstract homotopy theory
            in this sense.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Literature
            (
            %a{:href => "#DwyerSpalinski95"}> Dwyer-Spalinski 95
            )
          %p
            %br/
          #CategoryWithWeakEquivalences.num_defn
            %h6#definition_19 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
              is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %li
                %p
                  a sub-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi Mor
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \subset Mor(\mathcal{C})
                  of its
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            %p such that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                  contains all the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
                  of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                  is closed under
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-out-of-three"} two-out-of-three
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  of the form
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mi Y
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo ↗
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo ↘
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi X
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo ⟶
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mi Z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        \&amp;&amp; Y
                        \\\
                        \&amp; \nearrow&amp;&amp; \searrow
                        \\\
                        X
                        \&amp;&amp;
                        \\longrightarrow
                        \&amp;&amp;
                        Z
                        }
                %p
                  if two of the three morphisms are in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi W
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_8 Remark
            %p
              It turns out that a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryWithWeakEquivalences"}
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalences"} homotopy equivalences
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} simplicial localization
              of a category with weak equivalences (
              %a{:href => "simplicial+localization#DwyerKanLocalizations"}> Dwyer-Kan 80a
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "simplicial+localization#DwyerKanCalculating"} Dwyer-Kan 80b
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "simplicial+localization#DwyerKanFunctionComplexes"} Dwyer-Kan 80c
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              serve the sole purpose of making the universal homotopy theory induced by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
              be tractable:
          #ModelCategory.num_defn
            %h6#definition_20 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  with all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
                  (def.
                  = succeed ");" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LimitsAndColimits"}
              %li
                %p
                  three sub-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classes"} classes
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Fib
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Cof
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi Mor
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W, Fib, Cof \subset Mor(\mathcal{C})
                  of its
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            %p such that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the class
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                  makes
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  into a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryWithWeakEquivalences"}
              %li
                %p
                  The pairs
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi W
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi Cof
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi Fib
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (W \cap Cof\;,\; Fib)
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Cof
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi W
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi Fib
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Cof\;,\; W\cap Fib)
                  are both
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+systems"} weak factorization systems
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WeakFactorizationSystem"}
            %p One says:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  elements in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                  are
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
              %li
                %p
                  elements in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Cof
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cof
                  are
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
              %li
                %p
                  elements in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Fib
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fib
                  are
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
              %li
                %p
                  elements in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi Cof
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W\cap Cof
                  are
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+cofibrations"} acyclic cofibrations
              %li
                %p
                  elements in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi Fib
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \cap Fib
                  are
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+fibrations"} acyclic fibrations
          %p
            The form of def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ModelCategory"}
            is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Joyal"}> Joyal, def. E.1.2
            ). It implies various other conditions that (
            %a{:href => "#Quillen67"}> Quillen 67
            ) demands explicitly, see prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClosurePropertiesOfInjectiveAndProjectiveMorphisms"}
            and prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WeakEquivalencesAreClosedUnderRetracts"}
            below.
          %p
            We now dicuss the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+systems"} weak factorization systems
            appearing in def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ModelCategory"}
          %h3#WeakFactorizationSystems Factorization systems
          #LiftingAndExtension.num_defn
            %h6#definition_21 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
